  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Couple of rounds of showers and thunderstorms \npossible today.\n\nSurface low passing east across the Great Lakes early this morning \nwill continue to slide into southern Ontario and over Lake America \nalong a stationary boundary draped along the northern portions of \nthe forecast area today. A leading shortwave trough, ahead of the \nsurface low, will continue to support the showers diving southeast \nacross the North Country this morning as well as cause the \nshowers/thunderstorms across Michigan to continue to progress \neastward, reaching the Niagara Frontier region within the next few \nhours (so around sunrise). Within the western showers/thunderstorms \nthe air mass is supporting mid-level lapse rates of 6.5 to 7C/km, \nand an EML and effective shear of 25 to 30 kts due to a 35 knot jet \nat 700mb in place overhead the Great Lakes region. \n\nWestern and north central New York will remain under the warm sector \ntoday as the surface low skims by to the northwest. Modest heating \nand lapse rates will limit destabilization this afternoon. However, \nthe presence of dewpoints in the low-mid 60s and low-level hodograph \ncurvatures will favor organized convection to develop this \nafternoon. This being said, there is a LIMITED threat for localized \ndamaging wind gusts. SPC continues to promote a Marginal Risk for \nSevere Thunderstorms today, though as their forecast discussion \nstates \"...confidence in the magnitude and coverage of the threat \nremains low at this time.\" \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Colder weather by the end of the week with some \npotential for snow. \n\nA deepening upper level trough will progress eastward into the Great \nLakes region Thursday night and Friday. The associated surface low \nwill strengthen across the Great Lakes, supporting a sharp cold \nfront to sweep through on Friday. Confidence remains low at this \ntime as model discrepancies with the timing, strength and placement \nof this system persists. Regardless, the atmospheric pattern will \nlikely bring the potential for strong winds Friday into Saturday. \nAdditionally, a much colder airmass will surge into the area in the \nwake of the frontal passage with pre-frontal synoptic rain showers \neventually giving way to lake effect rain and snow showers.",
